# Rimefall Milestone 1.1 — controller feel fixes (from a real playtest: macOS Chrome, PS4 DualShock 4 over Bluetooth)

Context: Milestone 1 (your code, below) works in automated tests, but the real playtest showed bad controller feel. Diagnosis by the director: the camera yaw is ~43° (viewing from the south-east), so the tile grid appears rotated ~45° on screen, but input.js maps stick/WASD directly to grid axes. So stick-up moves along a grid axis, which looks diagonal/sideways on screen, and the 45° sector snapping flips between neighbours near sector boundaries (feels "too sensitive", causes zig-zag paths).

Make ONLY these changes. Output the COMPLETE contents of every file you change (=== FILE === format). Keep the module structure and keep all existing window.__rimefall fields.

1. Camera-relative movement (input + main/render): movement intent is a SCREEN direction. For each of the 8 grid directions, compute its projected screen-space direction using the actual camera (project the player's tile center and tile center + dir onto the screen; recompute when the camera/resize changes or just each tick — cheap). Snapping rule for a stick (or keyboard) screen angle:
   - Radial deadzone 0.35 on the left stick (axes 0/1; axis 1 positive = down on screen).
   - Define the 4 "screen-cardinal" grid directions as those whose projected screen angle is closest to screen up, right, down, left. Define the 4 "screen-diagonal" grid directions as the remaining ones.
   - If the stick angle is within ±15° of a screen diagonal (45°, 135°, 225°, 315° measured from screen-up), choose the grid direction whose projected angle is closest to that screen diagonal. Otherwise choose the screen-cardinal grid direction nearest to the stick angle (i.e. cardinals own a 60° wide sector each, diagonals 30°).
   - Apply the same mapping to WASD/arrows: W = screen up, S = screen down, A = left, D = right, two keys = the screen diagonal.
   - Sample the stick fresh at tick time (use the latest polled axes; no stale buffered direction from earlier frames). Releasing the stick before the tick means no move.
   - The D-pad (buttons 12-15) must NOT move the player; it only queues swap1/2/3 actions (14=swap1, 12=swap2, 15=swap3; button 13 unused for now).
   - Keep the existing walkability + no-corner-cutting rule for diagonal grid moves. If the chosen grid direction is blocked, do not move (no sliding).
   Put the direction-picking math in a small pure function (e.g. pickGridDir(screenAngle, projectedDirs)) so it is testable.
2. Straight diagonal glide: the mesh must interpolate in a straight line from the previous tile to the new tile (single lerp on x and z together), and face its actual movement direction (rotate toward the move vector; when idle keep the last facing).
3. Lower perceived latency without breaking the fixed 600 ms tick: keep the logical move on tick boundaries. When the player was idle and movement input begins mid-tick, immediately start a visual "pre-glide" of the mesh toward the predicted next tile (only if that tile is walkable per the same rules), covering at most ~35% of the distance by the time the tick fires; at the tick, the logical move commits and the mesh continues from its current visual position to the target tile, arriving at the end of that tick. If input is released or changed before the tick, ease the mesh back/redirect smoothly. Held movement must be continuous: consecutive moves glide tile to tile at constant speed with no pause or bob reset hitch between tiles.
4. Live controls hint (bottom-left #hint): it must swap live based on the last-used device. Keyboard: current text. Gamepad: if the gamepad id (case-insensitive) contains 'dualshock', 'dualsense', 'wireless controller', or '054c', show PlayStation names: "Left stick move · ✕ attack · □ eat · D-pad swap gear · Options pause". Otherwise Xbox names: "Left stick move · A attack · X eat · D-pad swap gear · Start pause". Mapping under standard mapping stays attack=button 0, eat=button 2, pause=button 9.
5. Connected toast: also show "🎮 Controller connected: <id>" when a pad was already connected before page load and is first seen by polling (no gamepadconnected event). Make sure the first detection triggers it exactly once per id.
6. Title: index.html <title>Rimefall — Milestone 1.1</title>.
7. Test hook additions on window.__rimefall: tileToScreen(x, z) -> {x, y} in CSS pixels (tile center on the floor); meshScreen() -> {x, y} of the rendered player mesh base; meshWorld() -> {x, z} of the rendered mesh; get hintText() -> current #hint text; lastMoveDir -> {x, z} grid dir chosen at the last tick (or null).

